WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.960
A three story building is about 10 meters tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.080
six times bigger than you. In the opposite&nbsp;
direction, six times smaller than you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:15.160
you get things like a cute squirrel about 27&nbsp;
centimetres small. So the building is just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:21.978
big relative to you as you are to a squirrel.&nbsp;
You're in the middle. It's easy to understand.

00:00:21.978 --> 00:00:27.760
In fact, you are in the middle of everything&nbsp;
in the Universe. Let's go on a fantastical

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.480
journey together, to the small and the&nbsp;
large and see if it's really true!

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:40.474
An A320 is 37 meters long. The&nbsp;Rufous 
hummingbird is around 7 centimetres.

00:00:40.474 --> 00:00:44.280
Both of these fliers are&nbsp;23 times 
bigger or smaller than you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.960
and both fly intercontinental distances - the&nbsp;
tiny bird migrates between Alaska and Mexico!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:57.480
If the hummingbird were the same size as the jet,&nbsp;
it would circle the Earth 85 times every year!

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.000
Dinoponera, the largest ant in the world, 
is about&nbsp;55 times smaller than you.

00:01:03.918 --> 00:01:10.680
 Their small colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;have around 100 individuals but no queens –&nbsp;instead they ruthlessly compete for status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:17.160
within the nest, which can reach 1.2 meters deep.&nbsp;
If humans lived like Dinoponera, we’d be building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:24.920
towers of over 25 stories filled with offices&nbsp;
and ruthlessly competing for status and...wait…

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:31.400
The deadliest and most annoying insect in the&nbsp;
world is the mosquito, 235 times smaller than you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.680
while on the other end the Empire state building&nbsp;
is about that much larger than you. Kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:42.960
unimaginable how something this small creates&nbsp;
so much devastation for something that big.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.480
We are getting to the borders&nbsp;
of human perception now.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.320
Like coarse grains of sand about 3&nbsp;
millimeters, 550 times smaller than you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.160
You can feel their shape and roughness&nbsp;
between your fingers and if you focus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.560
see them individually. We mix them into concrete&nbsp;
that can hold up the tallest towers ever built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:08.360
like the 828 meter tall Burj Khalifa that is 500&nbsp;
times larger than you. If you were that tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:14.760
people would be as small to you as grains&nbsp;
of sand in your hand. Hey! Be gentle.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.040
Anything smaller or bigger&nbsp;
and it becomes hard to grasp.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.120
A medium sized city like Lisbon&nbsp;
is about 6000 times larger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.280
yourself and permeated by a network&nbsp;
of highways, roads and alleys.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.240
On the other end, about 6000 times smaller&nbsp;
than you, are your small arteries permeating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.320
your whole body. Actually you are in the middle&nbsp;
between your network of blood vessels and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.600
network of a city like Lisbon. If you think of&nbsp;
a city as a living being, you find more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:51.480
parallels. A small alley is as small to the city&nbsp;
as an arteriole 0.1 millimeters wide is to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.588
Your tiniest capillaries are to you, what the&nbsp;
pipes bringing water to homes are to Lisbon.

00:02:57.404 --> 00:03:02.680
Going further, 100,000 times smaller than&nbsp;
you, we reach a typical skin cell about 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.480
micrometers in diameter, a Neutrophil is half as&nbsp;
big and one of your red blood cells is merely 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.160
micrometers. They are as small to you, as you&nbsp;
are to the entire Tokyo Metropolitan Area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.680
the largest urban area in the world spanning&nbsp;
over 160 km. You are so incredibly big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:26.160
filled with so much complexity, so many&nbsp;
different moving parts. Or are you just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.920
cell, in the human civilization&nbsp;
superstructure? Are you both?

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.240
Our steps are getting larger and larger now.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:40.600
Germany is around 875 km from North to South&nbsp;
and the 4th biggest agricultural exporter in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:47.840
world. Rhizobia is a nitrogen-fixing bacteria up&nbsp;
to 3 micrometers long and without it, that sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.880
agricultural production is impossible. So we have&nbsp;
a country and a bacterium depending on each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:01.040
and you are in the middle, both being roughly&nbsp;
550,000 times larger or smaller than you.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.200
What about the whole Earth? It’s&nbsp;
about 12,700 km in diameter;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:12.400
about 7.7 million times larger than you. On&nbsp;
the other side of the scale is Corynebacterium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.680
as little as 0.3 micrometers across, living&nbsp;
on your skin and eyes along with 100 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.414
other bacteria. More than ten times&nbsp;
more than there are humans on earth.

00:04:22.414 --> 00:04:26.360
Again you are in the center. Right&nbsp;
in the middle of something so large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:31.440
that our civilization is a mere scratch on its&nbsp;
surface, and something so small and numerous you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.840
never notice its presence even as it touches&nbsp;
you. Does that make you feel small, or big?

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.240
From here on out, your brain is breaking a bit.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:48.960
4 times wider than Earth is Neptune, a&nbsp;
cold blue gas giant 49,500 km wide. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:56.400
largest planet though is Jupiter, 140,000&nbsp;
km in diameter. A titanic abyss shrouded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.920
in terrible winds. You could drop Earth whole&nbsp;
into its depths and it would simply vanish.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.560
On the opposite scale we find&nbsp;
the deadly West Nile virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:13.160
50 nanometers in diameter. Or one step down,&nbsp;
the spike proteins on a coronavirus, that open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:20.520
up cells for its RNA payload – they are as small&nbsp;
to you as you are to the planet Jupiter. You are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:29.600
in the middle between gigantic planets and the&nbsp;
world of viruses. These tiny things. So deadly.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.840
Let that sink in – a tiny virus is&nbsp;
taking over and killing lung cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:40.760
up to 500 times larger than itself, with&nbsp;
the help of a tiny protein weapon. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:46.800
like you trying to kill a giant the size&nbsp;
of the Burj Khalifa with a screwdriver.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.640
But the real boss of the Solar System is the Sun.&nbsp;
Ten times bigger than Jupiter, a billion times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.840
larger than you, controlling all the planets&nbsp;
and source of all energy that drives life.&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.880
A billion times smaller than you, clearly&nbsp;
the boss of our body is a DNA strand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.880
containing all the information making&nbsp;
your life possible. You’re right in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.693
middle between the most important&nbsp;
factors keeping everything alive.

00:06:10.693 --> 00:06:14.600
From here on on things just&nbsp;
kind of stop making sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.440
A billion is already too much but now&nbsp;
everything just seems to mean ‘a lot’.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.480
The supermassive black hole at the&nbsp;
center of our galaxy, Sagittarius A*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:30.120
is 14.5 billion times bigger than you.&nbsp;
A hydrogen atom is 15.5 billion times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.680
smaller than you. Yeah ok sure. But the&nbsp;
thing is, we are not even close to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.720
done and it's not impossible to get&nbsp;
at least a sense of how these scale.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.040
The solar system is 22 trillion times larger&nbsp;
than you – On the other end of the scale is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.920
wavelength of low energy neutrinos released&nbsp;
from fusion reactions in our Sun – about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:55.120
a hundred trillion of them are passing through&nbsp;
you every single second, like ghosts a trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:00.200
times smaller than you, basically never hitting&nbsp;
any of the particles inside you. If you moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:05.000
through the solar system in a straight line&nbsp;
you probably wouldn't hit anything either.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.120
Although things are beginning&nbsp;
to get really weird now.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.360
A single proton at the heart of the&nbsp;
hydrogen atom is almost exactly 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.800
quadrillion times smaller than you.&nbsp;
If the proton were as big as you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.461
the hydrogen atoms would be&nbsp;
taller than twelve Mt. Everests.

00:07:21.461 --> 00:07:26.232
On the other end, we meet something that&nbsp;
just breaks human brains: the incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.232 --> 00:07:31.253
vastness of space, we just have no reference&nbsp;
for these distances at all. The distance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.253 --> 00:07:36.360
the closest star to earth, Alpha Centauri, is&nbsp;
not 1 quadrillion times in the other direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:43.408
from the tiny proton – but 24 quadrillion.&nbsp;
Space is just so large it is kind of mean.

00:07:43.408 --> 00:07:45.480
And it goes on like this.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:50.960
A quintillion times smaller than you is the&nbsp;
strange world of the quarks. The proton is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.760
not actually like a tiny ball but kind of&nbsp;
just a ripple on the surface of the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:02.560
of quarks. Every moment, countless quarks pop into&nbsp;
existence, along with their anti-particle enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:09.280
before doing furious battle and annihilating each&nbsp;
other in an instant. How many? Impossible to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.200
because the harder you look for them&nbsp;
the more quarks seem to appear. We’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.640
simplifying so much it’s like a lie anyway.&nbsp;
However we choose to illustrate this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:24.320
it's wrong. What actually is a quark, what&nbsp;
does it look like to human minds? Nobody knows.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:30.040
As you sit here, confused, let’s look up&nbsp;
again. The ocean of quarks in a proton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:34.080
inside a single atom of a single&nbsp;
cell of your body is as small to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:41.160
as you are compared to a sphere around 174 light&nbsp;
years across, containing about 16 thousand stars.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.680
And this is just a tiny speck of dust&nbsp;
to our galaxy. The Milky Way is close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.960
to one sextillion times larger&nbsp;
than you. At the opposite end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.800
we have particles a sextillion&nbsp;
times smaller than yourself;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:59.200
like the wavelength of high energy neutrinos&nbsp;
released when cosmic rays hit our atmosphere.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.200
We are getting to the end.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:08.840
The Observable Universe is 93 billion lightyears&nbsp;
in diameter. Close to a billion billion billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:18.400
human lengths. But it’s still finite. It’s&nbsp;
‘only’ 465,000 Milky Ways side by side. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:24.400
you were the size of our galaxy, the observable&nbsp;
Universe would only be a day’s drive across.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.040
On the other end of that scale, we have&nbsp;
the tiniest particle ever detected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.840
a proton travelling so close to the&nbsp;
speed of light it got squished into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:38.080
pancake. As small compared to you as the&nbsp;
whole Observable Universe is big to you.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.800
We are at the border of things that we have&nbsp;
evidence for – are you truly in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:47.680
of everything? The theoretical smallest&nbsp;
physical distance is the Planck length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.600
a hundred million times smaller than even the&nbsp;
pancake proton. But we don’t know if it's real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.520
only that our theories of the universe&nbsp;
break down here. Likewise on the other end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:02.320
does the bigness of the universe match&nbsp;
the smallness of the Planck length? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:08.600
actually the universe could be considerably&nbsp;
larger than that. But we will never know.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.320
Let’s go back and look at the dimensions again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:21.760
There are so many big things and so&nbsp;
many small things wrapped up in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.618
The universe seems to be exactly the&nbsp;
right size – with you in the middle.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:34.320
How do you try to get it exactly right,&nbsp;
say when you have to make a big choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.600
like buying a new mattress? You probably&nbsp;
compare trustworthy reviews and test out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:43.040
a few models. But what if you plan to spend&nbsp;
money on something you can’t just return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.680
the seller – and the decision doesn’t&nbsp;
just affect your aching back? GiveWell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.320
is an independent and rigorous resource for&nbsp;
a different kind of purchase: a donation.

